## 2026-08-18 05:24 — explorer/0.9.0: stamina headroom on FIELD moves (not the village); + fixed a latent MariaDB schema bug the deploy surfaced

WHAT. Bumped explorer 0.8.0 -> 0.9.0. Added a stamina *headroom* to the field
move gate: a `move`/`ride` is only offered when `stamina >= 1.5 x raw cost`
(MOVE_STAMINA_SAFETY), instead of the bare `stamina >= cost`. Move-only —
attack/use/etc. keep the raw cost.

WHY (and why NOT the advice's #1). advice.md ranked "stamina-gate the village
loop" first, on the theory that ungated village actions spam not_enough_stamina.
A DB drill-down REFUTED that before writing any code:
  - Village economy actions (sell/buy/brew/equip/spend_xp) produce ZERO
    not_enough_stamina across ALL history (they cost no stamina; they only ever
    fail on gold/xp/slot/item). Gating them is a provable no-op.
  - not_enough_stamina (45% of 0.8.0 errors) is 98% FIELD `move` (83,361) vs
    attack 1,042. So the advice's mechanism #2 (cost underestimation) was the
    right family but the wrong action — move, not attack.
  - Measured floor move cost = 20 (stamina drop 14/15 per successful move +
    5/6 regen), matching the known move_stamina=20; web/rime double it but are
    rare. So the base gate (>=20) is CORRECT for floor.
  - Yet moves fail at a *shown* stamina of 13-29 (median 23, p95 28) on 94%
    plain floor (web/rime = 0% of failures). The bot only issues a move it
    believes it affords, but the server rejects it — the signature of a ~1-tick
    STALE frame: the acted-on stamina reading is higher than the server's live
    value (a prior move's cost not yet reflected). The headroom absorbs that
    staleness so a stale-high reading still affords the step; otherwise the char
    rests (double-rate regen) instead of spamming a doomed move.

EXPECTED EFFECT. move/not_enough_stamina share falls sharply next window vs the
0.8.0 baseline; overall action-error rate drops toward M1's <5% exit criterion
(0.8.0 sat ~0.5). Early read on run #15 (~3 min in): 3 errors / 2057 actions
(0.15%) vs 0.8.0 ~0.27 err/action — promising but PRELIMINARY (fresh window,
chars still stamina-rich); judge on a multi-hour window.
FALSIFIED IF. move not_enough_stamina does NOT fall vs the 0.8.0 window, or the
overall error rate stays ~0.5. Watch exploration depth for throttling from the
extra rest ticks (if depth drops materially, lower the 1.5x).
RESIDUAL / DEEPER CAUSE. The staleness itself (bot acting on a frame ~1 tick
behind the server's applied state) is a client-layer issue; the headroom is a
robust decision-layer mitigation, not a client fix. Logged as a conjecture in
findings for a future iteration (drop stale frames / act only on the latest per
world).

--- unplanned but necessary: MariaDB schema splitter bug (be184e6) ---
The redeploy jumped the live bot forward across several committed-but-never-
deployed infra commits (the SQLite/MariaDB backend seam + config.toml, dashboard,
retention). config.toml selects type=mariadb, so the new runner tried to start on
MariaDB and CRASH-LOOPED at Storage() init: apply_schema -> executescript ->
_split_statements split the `decisions` DDL on a ';' that lives inside a `--`
comment ("... via SELECT DISTINCT;"), handing the driver a truncated CREATE TABLE
(1064). SQLite never hit it (native executescript). Root-cause fix: strip `--`
line comments before splitting on ';'. Verified: apply_schema now applies the full
schema cleanly against the live MariaDB (all 7 tables) and the env-gated
test_mariadb_roundtrip passes end-to-end. So the operator's staged MariaDB
migration is now UNBLOCKED.

