## 2026-08-18 11:54 — roster desync investigated (server shows chars intermittently); shipped low-noise anomaly self-reporting + an honest roster/inventory dashboard panel

**Trigger.** Operator saw the dashboard roster (7 = 5 home + 2 fielded) disagree
with the server's own map (28). Investigated.

**What the desync actually is (finding, recorded).** NOT a parsing bug. The
BotGuilds server presents characters INTERMITTENTLY — in run #32 a live char is
absent from every received frame for up to ~350 ticks (median max sighting gap
342t among long-lived chars) — and the guild's true roster is large and
PERSISTENT across runs (uids up to c7615, far more than the 13 recruits this
run). So a single frame's `guild` snapshot (`chars_here` + `chars_by_world`) is
an inherently PARTIAL view of a big persistent roster; the reported roster swings
30->6->30 while only 5 chars died. The server's map knows the full roster; our
frames carry only the currently-loaded subset.

**What I did NOT do, and why.** I prototyped a frame-based RosterTracker to
reconstruct the true count and gate recruit/embark on it. It does not work: no
window reliably recovers the count (30t: no change to the dip; 800t: stdev only
6.6->5.6 and dead chars then linger 800t). So I REVERTED that strategy change —
shipping a "fix" that doesn't fix anything violates the ethic — and left the live
strategy clean at explorer/0.10.0. The over-recruit it was meant to address is
already curbed by 0.10.0's recruit cooldown (roster_cap 87->11 in run #32).

**What I shipped instead (two honest, achievable pieces):**

1. **Low-noise anomaly self-reporting (`steemer/anomaly.py`, wired in
   `bot.on_action_error`).** The bot can't measure the true roster, but it can
   watch its own action-error stream and flag when a FAMILY spikes — the
   observable symptom of any desync (acting on a gone char / wrong world / full
   roster). `AnomalyMonitor`: a rolling per-reason window (300 ticks); a family
   with >=80 errors in it is flagged, re-reported at most once per 1200 ticks.
   Surfaced to stdout (live in the screen log) and a queryable `bot_anomaly`
   event (`Storage.record_anomaly`, kind=`bot_anomaly`, world=subtype), which the
   dashboard shows via `anomalies_recent` and the analysis loop folds into the
   notebook. The live bot deliberately does NOT write findings.jsonl directly —
   that is the loop's curated notebook, and a runtime writer would race the
   loop's rewrites, dirty the working tree, and (as a bug caught in review)
   pollute it from tests/replays. Calibrated on real data: 7 flags over
   run #28, 11 over run #32 — correctly pinpoints the chronic `unknown_character`
   field death-echo, and a NEW family regression would trip immediately. A quiet
   stream produces nothing.

2. **Honest roster + inventory dashboard panel** (`metrics.snapshot` now returns
   `roster` = {home, by_world, total, range{min,max,samples}, frame_age_s,
   inventory{kind:count}, note} and `anomalies_recent`; UI panel added in
   `ui/server.py`). The panel shows `total` as "now" ALONGSIDE the recent
   min-max range and the frame age, plus the note that it is a partial view of a
   larger persistent roster — so a dip reads as transient, not a lost roster.

**Bonus fix (found while adding the panel).** `metrics.snapshot()` took ~50s on
the live MariaDB — `SELECT MIN/MAX(received_at) FROM frames` full-scanned the
