## 2026-08-18 20:20 — INCIDENT + HOTFIX: server began zlib-compressing the ZeroMQ wire; bot crash-looped

While deploying 0.14.0 the bot went DOWN — every frame crashed with
`UnicodeDecodeError: byte 0x9c in position 1`. Diagnosis: the server started
**zlib-compressing wire messages mid-session** (0x78 0x9c is the zlib header);
run #38 played fine, then the restart hit the new format and `protocol.decode`'s
bare `json.loads` crashed on every frame. run-live.sh dutifully restarted the
crashing runner, burning run windows #39-#51 (each 0 frames) before diagnosis.

The server, guild (g_cd0e2a, 16 chars), and HTTP API were all up throughout — a
FOURTH guild (g_fd49e0, 30) also appeared — so it was purely our client not
speaking the new wire format.

Hotfix (git 7b4dbfe): `protocol.decode` now transparently `zlib.decompress()`es a
message that starts with 0x78, falling back to plain JSON. No change to `encode`
(our small uploads stayed accepted; the handshake worked, only inbound frames
were compressed). Deployed immediately (bot was down); run #52 now plays cleanly
(1000+ frames). pytest 197 passed incl. a compressed+plain decode test.

This is a SERVER PROTOCOL CHANGE, not a server bug — logged as a discovery.
Lesson: the periodic reference re-diff (check_submodule / check_webclient) should
also watch for wire-format changes; and a watchdog on "run window not advancing /
0 frames" (wishlist) would have caught the crash-loop instantly.

