CODEX PERF FIX + TESTING-GAP POST-MORTEM (operator asked how the tests missed the ~17s load).
FIX: api_codex now returns instantly (~0.13s) — serves cached codex or a `computing` placeholder
(mechanics pre-filled); the heavy 1200-frame decode runs in a BACKGROUND thread once per run_id
(cache + lock + inflight); client re-fetches on `computing`. Measured live: 1st 0.137s (was ~17s
at 1200 / ~55s at the shipped 4000), cached 0.11s. Committed 1e3a366, pushed, dash restarted.
WHY THE TESTS MISSED IT (3 compounding reasons):
  1. FIXTURES ARE TINY. Every codex test/smoke used a 1-FRAME seeded SQLite DB. api_codex cost is
     O(frames decoded via LIMIT sample); with 1 frame that's ~0ms. So the tests proved CORRECTNESS
     (sections populate) but by construction could NEVER see the SCALE cost. Seeded-DB frontend
     tests are BLIND to performance.
  2. BUMPED THE COST PARAM AFTER THE ONLY LIVE RUN. My one live api_codex run was at sample=1500 and
     I checked the OUTPUT, not the TIME. I then raised the default 1500->4000 (for monster behaviour)
     and re-validated only on the seeded DB + reaper (both 1-frame). The parameter that sets the cost
     changed after the last real-data run.
  3. NO LATENCY ORACLE. Nothing asserted/measured response time; correctness tests pass at any speed.
LESSON (durable): for ANY endpoint whose cost scales with data volume, TIME it against the LIVE DB
before shipping — especially after changing a sample/limit/window param — because the seeded fixtures
make it look instant. A correctness oracle is not a performance oracle. (Also: prefer off-thread +
cache for heavy dashboard endpoints from the start; the codebase already had that pattern for
/api/snapshot and I should have mirrored it up front.)

