ITER 58 (2026-08-21) — OUTAGE recovery + the supervision lever (no strategy change; how-nav shipped).
FOUND EVERYTHING DOWN at 01:49. bot/web/dash all stopped (run/ emptied ~01:45); restarting the bot put it in
a SILENT crash-loop: exit 139 (SIGSEGV) every start, run-live.sh respawning forever while `svc.sh status`
would have said "bot up". ROOT CAUSE (operator confirmed mid-pass: "I just did a freebsd update"): the FreeBSD
update replaced /usr/local/bin/python3.15 (3.15.0rc1, built Aug 18); uv noticed the interpreter changed,
DELETED and rebuilt .venv, and reinstalled its CACHED pyzmq wheel — a local scikit-build-core build compiled
against the OLDER 3.15 prerelease. Bisected to zmq_getsockopt(ZMQ_TYPE): `import zmq` fine, Context fine,
backend Socket fine, `s.get(zmq.TYPE)` SEGV. FIX: rebuilt pyzmq from source against the live interpreter
(uv pip install --no-cache --no-binary pyzmq --force-reinstall) and PURGED the poisoned cache entry
(uv cache clean pyzmq --force; the running services hold a shared cache lock, so --force was needed).
Bot back up on run #113 at 02:0x, ~12 frames/s, staleness 0.2s. Full local suite 341 green afterwards —
nothing else in the FreeBSD update broke (mysql-connector is pure-python).
WHY IT LASTED: nothing was watching. steemer/watchdog.py has always been able to answer "are frames
flowing?" but had NO CALLER (the cron gap flagged in HANDOFF); `svc.sh status` answers "is a pid alive?",
which a crash-loop satisfies. That is the lever this pass.
SHIPPED (infrastructure only — explorer.py UNTOUCHED, version deliberately NOT bumped so run #113 keeps
accumulating a clean 0.45 window and attribution stays honest; no redeploy needed):
 * steemer/health.py — PURE plan_action/plan/overall_level with an explicit clock (mirrors
   watchdog.classify_liveness) + thin IO. Precedence is the design: ok->none; warn->HOLD (a gap under the
   dead threshold is a hiccup; restarting costs a run window for nothing); broken venv->REPAIR, outranking
   restart (restarting is exactly what run-live.sh already does forever, to no effect); within cooldown->
   HOLD (no storms); else restart. Sidecar gets its OWN looser thresholds (it writes intel ~30s; the bot
   writes many frames/s — one shared threshold would either cry wolf on web or go blind on bot). The zmq
   smoke test runs OUT OF PROCESS on purpose: the failure being detected is a segfault, so an in-process
   check would take the supervisor down with it.
 * tools/healthcheck.py — the caller the watchdog never had. One-shot JSON + exit code (0/1/2) for a cron,
   or --watch/--fix as a service. NOW RUNNING: `./svc.sh up watch` (4th service, 60s cadence).
 * svc.sh — ROOT CAUSE of the long-standing "svc.sh down bot leaves steemer.runner alive" gotcha, finally
   named: daemon(8) records the CHILD pid in the pidfile but the group LEADER is daemon itself, so
   `kill -TERM -$pid` signalled a NONEXISTENT group and the `|| kill $pid` fallback killed only run-live.sh,
   orphaning uv+python (live proof: bot pid 2510, pgid 2508). Now resolves the real pgid, kills the
   descendant tree, and REPORTS survivors instead of swallowing them. Three guards, each earned: (a) stale/
   recycled pidfile refused via a per-service cmdline marker; (b) refuses to signal its OWN process group —
   found for real when mutation-testing (a) made svc.sh kill the pytest runner; (c) existence now comes from
   POSIX `kill -0`, not ps — the reaper gate (slim image, no procps) caught the old check reporting a LIVE
   service "stale" and exiting 0, a stop command failing OPEN. status now reports health, not just a pid.
 * run-live.sh — still restarts forever (the game must keep playing) but writes run/bot.crashloop after N
   consecutive sub-FAST_FAIL_S failures, and RESETS both the tally and the backoff after a run that really
   played (the backoff previously stayed pinned at 30s for the process's life).
 * .reaper.toml — installs procps in [run] so the gate exercises the above instead of skipping it.
ALSO SHIPPED (wishlist top item, 0.5026 — see table): the "How nav works" tab. Deliberately DERIVED, not
written down, which was the operator's actual ask: the map rules come from steemer/nav.py at request time
(live DIRS/SOLID + inspect.getdoc of the functions the planner calls), and the priority ladder comes from the
