0.52.0 FORGING — the M3a chain closed, and the blocker was never real. docs/07 deferred forging because the
`product` name was "unpublished" and a blind guess storms unknown_product. It was in our OWN database the
whole time: 189 rival forged/forge_started events name it outright. shield_iron leads the ladder because it
is armour the shop sells at NO price and 100% of our chars have an empty offhand — the one product where
forging is not the cheaper route but the only one.
RECIPE QUANTITIES ARE STILL UNDOCUMENTED, so it learns by rejection: an ordered ladder, and every rejected
(product, ingots, lumber) blacklisted. Run #129 duly LEARNED them from the server's own wrong_materials:
  spear = 1 ingot + 1 lumber   dagger = 1 + 1   shield_iron = 3 ingots + 1 lumber (2+2 refused)
All five `forged` events on #129 were OURS (spear x2, dagger x2, shield_iron x1). 15 forge actions,
6 wrong_materials + 2 no_forge_nearby — the errors ARE the documentation.

...AND THEN THE VERIFICATION FOUND THE OUTPUT WAS WORTHLESS. Filtering the `sale` feed to our own guild_id
showed spear, dagger and shield_iron going straight back over the counter. Forging was value-DESTROYING.
The chain was mechanical and nothing about it was a 0.52 regression: _equip_action filled only EMPTY slots,
so the brute-force slot search tried the forged spear in outfit/trinket/boots and learned all three were
wrong_slot (20 of the 20 equip errors on the run); that left `hand`, holding a 15-gold club; and _should_sell
keeps gear "only while a slot it could still go into remains", which was now false. The sell rule long
predates the forge — 0.52 merely produced the first items that ever met it.

0.53.0 EQUIP-UPGRADE — gear may displace WORSE gear. Ranked on the shop's own buy_price, learned live from
stock, because it is the only ranking the game exposes: items carry no damage or armour number, and `tier`
is NOT ordered (#129 forged a tier-0 spear beside a tier-1 club). Three conditions, each earned by a failing
test rather than by theory: SAME CLASS (weapon vs armour — ranking across classes proposed evicting a 25g
shield with a 70g spear, which a test caught); STRICTLY dearer (equal churns forever, and this is also what
stops a kind displacing itself); BOTH prices KNOWN (an unseen price is not zero).
NAMED GAP, not a silent one: shield_iron is sold at no price, so it can never be SWAPPED in — only worn into
an offhand still empty. Valuing the unbuyable would be a guess, and is the next slice.
Swapping into an occupied slot is UNDOCUMENTED and may not exist, so it is learn-by-rejection too:
SWAP_GIVE_UP=3 distinct refusals concludes there is no swap, and the sell guard lapses with it so held gear
never becomes permanent dead weight. wrong_slot routes to the slot map and deliberately spends no refusal.

THE HARNESS LIED AGAIN, AND THAT MATTERS MORE THAN THE FEATURE. tools/mutate.py infers "killed" from a
nonzero pytest exit — and pytest also exits nonzero when the selector matches NOTHING. Two mutants this pass
were reported KILLED against test names I had never written. A harness whose whole job is to prove tests can
fail must not accept "there was no test" as proof. It now runs each selector CLEAN first and refuses to score
it unless it passes unmutated; verified by feeding it a deliberately nonexistent selector, which it SKIPs.
This is the second time this harness has silently lied (the first was the bytecode-mtime trap, same session).
Both times the failure was the oracle, not the code — self-test the oracle.

TESTS: 24 new (equip-upgrade), 10 (forge). 21 mutants killed on 0.53 after THREE rounds; the first round's
survivors were all my own weak tests — a fixture that left the offhand empty, a kill-switch assertion
confounded by its own blacklist, an unclassified kind blocked for the wrong reason — plus one guard that
mutation proved genuinely redundant, which is now DELETED rather than tested around.
