iter 69 — 2026-08-21 — ORE: a lever that shipped INERT, then two regressions, then a win
================================================================================
Runs #131-#134. Four deploys, and the first three were wrong. Worth recording in that order
because the lesson is in the sequence, not the destination.

MEASUREMENT: run #130 put the M3a bottleneck beyond doubt — 120 trees destroyed against 5
veins, lumber piling up while ore trickled. NOT scarcity: the accumulated map knows 85 vein
tiles in the mines and HALF our char-frames are already in the mines. DENSITY. Slice 1
(v0.45.0) harvests only what a char is already ADJACENT to; vale's 357 trees are thick
enough to brush past constantly, 85 veins among ~4,900 mine floor tiles are not.

0.54.0 SEEK ORE — and it SHIPPED INERT. Zero firings in 7,714 frames, against 1,024
adjacent-harvest offers in the same window. Not outscored; never OFFERED. I had validated it
against the accumulated map read from the tiles_seen TABLE (72% of random floor tiles have a
vein in reach) — but `bot.known` starts EMPTY every run and tiles_seen was written on every
frame and NEVER READ BACK. I validated against an input the running system does not have.
Same shape as the 0.51.0 segfault, where the missing input was a caller.

0.55.0 HYDRATE the map at startup — correct, and it REGRESSED the bot 4x. Move failures
5.2% -> 19.4% of moves. `ctx.containers` is derived from `known`, so hydrating `known`
silently promoted chest-beelining (4.5) from a local errand to a map-wide one: chars set off
across the whole remembered map toward chests from earlier runs. Attributed properly by
joining each move_failed to the SAME char's own decision that tick — the first attempt,
joined on tick alone, was too noisy and blamed gold-beelining.

0.56.0 scoped WHICH chests count (contents are not durable; terrain is) — RIGHT DISTINCTION,
WRONG SIZE. Only 19.4% -> 21.0%. The surviving chests were ones seen THIS run, and a
complete terrain map makes those reachable from anywhere too.

0.57.0 BOUND field errands to FIELD_GOAL_RANGE=20. Every field goal search was unbounded,
harmless only by accident: pre-hydration a char knew a few hundred tiles, so "nearest chest"
was necessarily near and usually had no known corridor anyway. RESULT on #134, a clean win:
  move failures 21.0% -> 1.5% (BELOW the 5.2% pre-hydration baseline)
  chest-beelines 0.980 -> 0.046 per frame (pre-hydration was 0.047 — restored exactly)
  vein-seek still live at 0.120/frame; trees destroyed 129 in 6,239 frames vs 131 in 40,167
  on #130 — roughly SEVEN TIMES the harvest rate.

THE NEAR-MISS THAT MATTERS MOST: `_retreat` walks HOME through the same helper (goal y==0),
and chars range to y=126 in mines, y=199 in vale. Bounding it would leave a hurt char unable
to find a route home and offering `rest` — the exact stuck-death v0.42.0 and v0.50.0 were
both spent on. THE FULL SUITE PASSED WITH THAT BUG IN. Nothing pinned the retreat's
long-range requirement, so re-opening the project's worst failure mode looked green. There
is now a test that walks a char home from 100 tiles out.

ALSO FOUND, NOT FIXED: 201 failures on #132 were moves OUT OF BOUNDS in vale. nav.frontier
counts a neighbour absent from `known` as unexplored, and beyond the map edge nothing ever
will be — so every edge tile is a permanent false frontier. Harmless while genuine interior
remained; vale is now ~100% mapped. Needs the frame's `bounds` plumbed into nav.
STILL OPEN: 0 veins destroyed yet on #134 (129 trees). Vein-seek fires but has not converted.
INFRA: `reaper up` now HANGS INDEFINITELY AFTER printing its success line (~45 min lost
across two occurrences). `reaper test` against the already-up session is fine. Logged in
/home/cal/reaper_bugs.md with the workaround and a warning that killing `up` also kills the
heartbeat child.

