MEASURED FIRST — 0.58.0's bottles WORKED, and the chain moved a link. Run #135, 33,388
frames: 8 bottles bought, 9 brews (ZERO in the previous 31,011 frames), potion_red carried
0.0% -> 1.81%, and mines depth max jumped 7 -> 40. A healed character walked straight past
the POISON_SAFE_DEPTH cap iter 70 traced. Mechanism confirmed end to end.

Thin, though: only 1 of 9 brews was potion_red, because a vigor batch needs two vigor
ingredients in one pack — and the same run SOLD the ingredients. 2 `bone` (a vigor herb, the
only route to potion_red) and 5 `ore_copper` (raw forge feedstock) went over the counter
under the stranded-singleton rule. That rule is right for abundant things and exactly wrong
for a scarce input: a singleton is not clutter, it is HALF A PAIR, and selling it guarantees
it stays half a pair forever. Note the ore case was a plain gap — FORGE_FEEDSTOCK_PREFIXES
covers `ingot` but not the `ore` an ingot is smelted from.

0.59.0 keeps both, bounded at SCARCE_LONE_KEEP=2 per KIND per char so it can never become
the carry-clog the original rule prevents. Vigor membership is read from knowledge.ESSENCE,
never duplicated here, so a newly decoded vigor herb is covered without a code edit. Four
existing tests changed and none weakened: two used `bone` merely as a stand-in for "a
sellable thing" while actually testing ordering and the re-send cooldown (now pure loot);
the two ore tests were rewritten to the new policy with the anti-clog intent preserved as an
explicit cap assertion. GATE 667 reaper / 659 local; 9 new tests, 12 mutants killed (plus a
deliberate no-op control that correctly SURVIVED).

THEN I MISDIAGNOSED, AND THE CORRECTION IS THE USEFUL PART. Run #136 sent ONLY `move` and
