and `(spear,1,1)` produced `forged` events on #129 AND #140. We had condemned a recipe we
watched work, twice.

CAUSE: v0.52.0 abandoned a recipe on its FIRST `wrong_materials`, assuming the refusal is a
function of (product, ingots, lumber). Pairing each forge action with the item KINDS it
spent refutes that — identical product, kinds and quantities both succeed and fail inside
one run. Against a non-deterministic signal a one-strike permanent blacklist is a RATCHET:
refusals only ever remove options, so the forge trends to inert however well it works.
FIX: PROOF OUTRANKS REFUSAL. A `forged` event credits the recipe that character last
attempted (the only positive evidence this ladder gets); a proven recipe is never
blacklisted and accrues no strikes; an unproven one now needs 3 refusals, not 1; and proof
retroactively clears a wrongful abandonment — the `(spear,1,1)` repair. Scoped to our eids
(rivals forge constantly; 189 such events in our history).
HONEST GAP: I do not know WHY identical materials sometimes fail. Tier is the suspect (items
carry one; we ignore it) and my failure attribution uses a +/-3 tick window so some labels
may be wrong. The fix needs neither — only that refusal is unreliable and success is not.

A MEASUREMENT I NEARLY SHIPPED ON. A stateless replay claimed 680 forge opportunities missed
against 18 taken (~97%). ARTIFACT: it used a FRESH strategy per frame, which does not know
what the live one has learned. Re-running the whole run through ONE stateful instance
reproduced the live counts almost exactly (forge 18 vs 18, smelt 11 vs 11) and pointed at
