Then the audit the HANDOFF called for, applied to every latch in explorer.py.

`wont_fit` was the worst, because the gate it models is one WE DELIBERATELY MOVE. `spend_xp`
has fired 2,151 times; between #129 and #141 our maxima went str 2->6, vit 3->8, end 3->6,
level 6->18. A kind refused at str 2 stayed refused at str 6 FOREVER, in all seven places it
is consulted — equipping (x2), the armour buy, the forge product ladder, and the SELL rule,
which then banks the item. 19 such refusals have accumulated over the project's life.
A SECOND fault sat on top: keyed on KIND alone the set was GLOBAL, so the weakest character
to try a bow decided whether anyone could wear one — exactly backwards.
0.65.0 records the BAR (the stat total refused) instead of a bare no. Out-grow it and you may
retry; a second refusal raises the bar (so a grown char doesn't retry every visit); a WEAKER
refusal can never lower it. Where no character is in hand (sell rule, forge ladder) the
question becomes "has the BEST of us out-grown it" — a kind one character can wear must not
be banked. NAMED PROXY: the frame never says WHICH stat was short, so the sum stands in; a
char that grew the wrong stat gets one more refusal, costing one action.
0.65.1 applied the identical release to `_tome_failed` (INT-gated, INT grows).

AUDIT COMPLETE, with reasons for the ones left alone:
  released: wont_fit (0.65.0), _tome_failed (0.65.1), _forge_failed (0.64.0)
  fine as-is: slot_wrong (a bow never goes on your feet — a fixed property, not a threshold),
              _swap_failed (same, plus SWAP_GIVE_UP bounds the behaviour), STUCK_BLOCK (TTL),
