iter 83 — 2026-08-22 — the map edge is not a frontier (0.70.0, run #149)
================================================================================
0.69.0 CONFIRMED: potion_red coverage 4.1% -> 27.34% (9 bought), and the "no heal past the
safe depth" retreat fell 10.2% -> 7.3% of decisions. AND DEPTH DID NOT MOVE — mines median
still 2, p90 6. Chasing that found the trap.

`nav.frontier` counted any neighbour absent from `known` as unexplored. Beyond the map edge
nothing is EVER in `known`, so every rim tile called itself a permanent frontier — and
`bfs_step` returns the NEAREST goal. On the real map:
    mines: 126 "frontier" tiles, 58 OF THEM AT y=0 (the home row)
    genuine frontier at depth 89-126; veins at median depth 88; OUR CHARS at median depth 2
A character at depth 2 always found a fake frontier one tile away, stepped along the rim, and
repeated. The behaviour meant to push characters OUTWARD was pinning them in place, and it
fed itself: staying shallow kept the false frontier nearest.
0.70.0 plumbs the frame's `bounds` into nav.frontier. Optional throughout, so village frames,
replays and older shapes keep the old behaviour rather than raising. Live effect: mines
126 -> 68 frontier tiles (remaining median depth 118), vale 526 -> 12 (depth 197). 572 false
frontiers gone.
I FLAGGED THIS IN ITER 69 AND DEFERRED IT AS SMALLER. It was the binding constraint on depth.

STATED PLAINLY, NOT BURIED: removing a false attractor is not adding a true one. The genuine
frontier at depth 89+ is far outside FIELD_GOAL_RANGE=20, so characters now find NO frontier
rather than a fake one. Honest, and it stops wasted motion, but it may simply raise the
already-high rest share (55%). WATCH: idle share. The sized follow-on is ready — the
SHALLOWEST vein is depth 24 against VEIN_SEEK_RANGE=14, so the nearest ore is just out of
reach of a char at depth 2, and with 27% now carrying a heal a longer ore errand is
defensible in a way it was not last week.

A NEGATIVE RESULT WORTH KEEPING — I did not change MOVE_STAMINA_SAFETY. 55% of decisions are
`rest`, and the trace says "wanted move ... but stamina 18 < ~30: resting", which looked like
a 1.5x margin wasting half our ticks. It is correctly calibrated: moves STILL fail
not_enough_stamina at shown-stamina up to 29-30 (median 26-28) on #147/#148, exactly as
v0.9.0 measured. A move costs ~15 and resting regenerates 10-12/tick, so resting most ticks is
INHERENT to the stamina economy. The constant stays, with fresh evidence attached. Two
constants re-tested this week: one had expired (POTION_RESERVE), one had not. Re-testing is
worth it precisely because you cannot tell which without looking.
GATE 796 reaper / 787 local; 10 tests, 8 mutants killed (two on the PLUMBING).

