iter 86 — 2026-08-22 — five executable guardrails against the LOOP's failure rate
================================================================================
No bot change this pass. The operator asked what I could do better and what we could write to
make it stick. The honest diagnosis: the code I ship is usually correct; what fails is knowing
whether it RAN and whether the numbers MEAN anything. In 24h: 6 attribution errors (11 across
project history), 4 correct-but-unreachable versions, 7 test-craft slips, 2 expired premises.

AND PROSE HAS ALREADY BEEN TRIED. `orchestrator/loop.md` has carried a "Metric attribution"
section since before go-live; memory already warned that death queries count rivals. Eleven
attribution errors happened anyway, one of which reached the operator as a confirmed result
and had to be retracted. Everything that ever STUCK in this project was executable —
shadow.MIN_DECISIONS, the mutate.py self-test, the pre-commit hook. So all five are executable.

 1. steemer/attribution.py — ours_only DEFAULTS TRUE; TooEarly under 20k frames; compare()
    cannot yield a delta without the band confounder; distinct_entities() answers "how many"
    not "how often". On #141 it returns 4 forges of ours against the 13 world-wide reported.
 2. steemer/claims.py + tools/recheck_claims.py — the 0.61.0 expectation detector pointed at
    the loop. A claim records the QUESTION, re-run against immutable history; disagreement can
    only mean the original was wrong. Only vetted checks recordable, else the ledger launders
    the same bad query. Three-valued (unavailable != contradicted).
 3. tests/test_reachability.py — a new offer fails the suite until its reachability is stated.
    Honest limit: makes the question unavoidable, does not prove a behaviour fires.
 4. tests/test_test_hygiene.py — ratchets on derived fixtures (caught 4x) and hand-rolled bot
    doubles (3x).
 5. tests/test_premises.py — `# PREMISE(date, claim): query`, stale after 45 days.
ALL BUDGETS RATCHET DOWN ONLY. Converted one behaviour immediately (vein-seek now driven
through GuildBot.on_frame), exemptions 23 -> 22, so the ratchet is seen turning, not described.
loop.md's "Metric attribution" section rewritten from advice into pointers at things that fail.

